#comparision operators.............(==) compares the values on either side of the operator and checks for equality. If both values are equal, it returns True; otherwise, it returns False.

a = 10 
b = 20
c = a==b
print(c)

#another example
a = 10
b = 10
c = a==b
print(c)

#(!=) compares the values on either side of the operator and checks for inequality. If both values are not equal, it returns True; otherwise, it returns False.
a = 10
b = 20
c = a!=b
print(c)

#(>) compares the values on either side of the operator and checks if the left value is greater than the right value. If it is, it returns True; otherwise, it returns False.
num1 = 30
num2 = 20
c = num1 > num2
print(c)

#(<) compares the values on either side of the operator and checks if the left value is less than the right value. If it is, it returns True; otherwise, it returns False.
num1 = 30
num2 = 20
c = num1 < num2
print(c)

#(>=) compares the values on either side of the operator and checks if the left value is greater than or equal to the right value. If it is, it returns True; otherwise, it returns False.
num1 = 10
num2 = 20
c = num1 >= num2
print(c)

#another example
num1 = 20
num2 = 20
c = num1 >= num2
print(c)

#(<=) compares the values on either side of the operator and checks if the left value is less than or equal to the right value. If it is, it returns True; otherwise, it returns False.
